## Idempotent Guarantees

Use composite keys (customerId:YYYY-MM) for DynamoDB upserts and check-if-not-exists patterns before writing. The distributed lock prevents concurrent finalizations for the same customer-month.

## Why This Is Asked
HashiCorp needs engineers who understand distributed database design at scale, especially for high-traffic scenarios. This tests knowledge of DynamoDB's partitioning model, hot key mitigation, and multi-region consistency trade-offs.

## Expected Answer
Strong candidates would discuss:
- Composite partition keys with random suffixes to distribute load
- Adaptive capacity for hot items during flash sales
- Trade-offs between eventual consistency and read-after-write
- Use of DynamoDB Accelerator (DAX) for read performance
- Consideration of global tables vs. application-level replication

## Code Example
```typescript
// Partition key strategy with sharding
const generatePartitionKey = (productId: string, shardCount: number = 10) => {
  const hash = productId.split('').reduce((acc, char) => acc + char.charCodeAt(0), 0);
  const shardId = hash % shardCount;
  return `${productId}#shard${shardId}`;
};

// Item structure for hot item handling
interface CatalogItem {
  PK: string; // product_id#shardX
  SK: string; // METADATA
  productId: string;
  shardId: number;
  // ... other attributes
  hotItemFlag: boolean; // for adaptive capacity
}
```

